  <result aggregation-status="0" type="1">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Tier 2 Results</narrative>
   </title>
   <indicator aggregation-status="0" ascending="0" measure="1">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Emergency Assistance</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">Cyclone Remal, which made landfall on 26 May 2024, was the most devastating cyclone to strike Bangladesh in recent years. According to the Joint Needs Assessment, there were 1.3 million people in need of assistance, of which approximately 364,000 were women of reproductive age including 17,492 pregnant women.    
&#x200d;
With support from DFAT, UNFPA quickly accessed prepositioned sexual and reproductive health supplies to meet the critical needs of cyclone-affected women and girls. UNFPA distributed nearly 1,500 menstrual health management to adolescent girls in areas which were very hard to access. During the distributions &#x2013; which were supported by female youth volunteers &#x2013; young girls were educated on menstrual hygiene and reproductive health. They were also given access to information and, where needed, referrals to support services on child marriage prevention, the prevention of sexual exploitation and abuse (PSEA), and gender-based violence (GBV). 
&#x200d;
 UNFPA Bangladesh also distributed 240 kits of essential hygiene supplies for newborn babies. Baby kits were distributed to pregnant women and new mothers through health and family welfare centres. Women were provided access to maternal and child healthcare, and encouraged to deliver their babies at a health facility under the care of a skilled birth attendant.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2024-07-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2025-06-30"></period-end>
    </period>
   </indicator>
  </result>
